Fidelity decisions worth knowing about:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- Output goes through C's [38;5;10mprintf[0m via an [38;5;10mextern[0m declaration rather than Rust's [38;5;10mprintln![0m. This keeps formatting ([38;5;10m%04X[0m, [38;5;10m%s[0m) and stdout buffering identical to the original, including interleaving if a C caller also writes to stdout.[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mLOG_VALUE(var)[0m stringifies its argument name, and [38;5;10mexecute_operation[0m's parameters are named [38;5;10ma[0m and [38;5;10mb[0m, so those lines are literally [38;5;10mVariable a = %d[0m / [38;5;10mVariable b = %d[0m.[0m[0m
- Signed overflow and shift behavior in [38;5;10mmultiply_with_static[0m, [38;5;10madd_with_static[0m, and [38;5;10mshift_with_static[0m is UB in C. I reproduced what the C compiler actually emits on x86-64: wrapping arithmetic, a wrapping left shift, and an arithmetic (sign-extending) right shift.[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mcheckshift[0m still uses [38;5;10mmalloc[0m/[38;5;10mfree[0m rather than a [38;5;10mBox[0m, so the [38;5;10mNULL[0m allocation-failure path exists as written.[0m[0m
- The final result keeps the C's implicit [38;5;10mint ^ unsigned int[0m promotion, computing in [38;5;10mu32[0m and casting back to [38;5;10mi32[0m.[0m[0m
